  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper level trough and associated cold front are \nslowly progressing east northeast through the region with scattered \nshowers across portions of the area. Wrap around moisture along the \nconvergent deformation band in northern New York will likely lead to \nsome continued light shower chances through this afternoon. In \nVermont, a boundary of showers and thunderstorms has exited east of \nthe Connecticut River Valley and along with it, most of our \ninstability for any stronger storms. Our marginal severe threat has \nended as well as a result. Behind the boundary, some lingering \nshowers will be possible, with perhaps an isolated rumble of thunder \nor two. Mainly, a quick half inch of rain will be possible in \nassociation with showers. Associated with the cold front, breezy, to \nlocally gusty, winds are expected along the higher terrain and \nChamplain Valley this afternoon/evening. As the front swings east, \nan associated strengthening 40-50 knot low level jet will move along \nthe northern portions of the region. Gusts on summit levels could \ngust up to 30-40 MPH, with valley winds around 20-30 MPH, mainly \nbetween 6 and 10 PM. A nocturnal inversion and eastward progression \nof the system will help temper down winds after midnight. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms will taper off for most by late this \nafternoon. Lingering northwest wrap around flow could lead to some \ncontinued stubborn shower chances along the higher terrain of the \nnorthern Greens and Adirondacks tonight. Across eastern Vermont, fog \ndevelopment appears likely in the valleys of the region with brief \nhigh pressure nudging in from the west, and with calming winds. \nTuesday and Wednesday will be quieter and calmer with temperatures \nin the upper 70s to mid 80s and mostly clear skies. Dewpoints will \nslowly creep up by mid week however, approaching the mid 60s in some \nplaces. With upper level diffluence in place from the high pressure, \nsome decent mid-level forcing will coincide with decent moisture \nadvection to bring scattered to numerous showers to the region \nThursday afternoon with the potential for a few strong thunderstorms \nshould forcing align with peak diurnal heating.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: No significant changes have been made to the forecast \nfor the end of the week into the weekend, with warm and unsettled \nweather expected as the region remains situated between a Bermuda \nhigh to our east and troughiness across the Plains. Broad \nsouthwesterly flow across the eastern US will allow for plentiful \nmoisture to advect into to the region, with occasional shortwaves \npassing overhead helping to drive shower chances. As usual in this \ntime frame, there is some uncertainty in the overall extent and \ntiming of precipitation, but several rounds of showers and possible \nthunderstorms look likely at this point. With fairly weak forcing \nand limited instability, severe weather looks unlikely however areas \nof locally heavy rain will be possible with PWAT values near 2 \ninches, although there should be enough flow to limit any flood \nthreat but trends will need to be monitored as we get closer. \nTemperatures during this time frame look to be on the warmer side, \nwith highs climbing into the 80s areawide this weekend, with \nincreased humidity making it feel a little uncomfortable at times.",
